<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stans</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Stans is the capital of the Swiss canton of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nidwalden">Nidwalden</a>, at the foot of the Stanserhorn south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-lucerne">Lake Lucerne</a>. In 1481 the Diet of Stans, helped by the mediation of the hermit <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niklaus-von-flue">Niklaus von Flüe</a>, settled a crisis that threatened to split the Confederacy. After the French massacre of 1798, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-heinrich-pestalozzi">Johann Heinrich Pestalozzi</a> ran an orphanage there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stans.jpg" alt="Stans" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Scoutboy · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stans_rathaus_3_thm.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Stans is one of the oldest settlements in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nidwalden">Nidwalden</a> valley, with the first traces of human settlement dating to the second century BCE. The Roman era left little evidence except some funeral pyres and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> root of its name, stagnum, meaning a pool. The Alamanni settled the region in the seventh or eighth century, and the Christian Alamanni built the first church in Stans around 750, which served as parish church for the whole Engelberg Valley until the church of St Peter and Paul replaced it in 1647. The town covers 11.08 square kilometres, its highest point on the Stanserhorn.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Stans was first mentioned in 1124. By 1291, when Unterwalden joined Uri and Schwyz in what became the Swiss Confederation, it was already the capital of Unterwalden below the forest, which became the half canton of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nidwalden">Nidwalden</a>. Tradition places a soldier from Stans, Arnold von Winkelried, at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sempach">Battle of Sempach</a> in 1386, where he is said to have thrown himself on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> pikes. It is doubtful that he existed, since the first mention of the act comes over 150 years later, but the legend is firmly kept in Stans, with a monument and a house bearing his name.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundian-wars">Burgundian Wars</a> the Confederation was nearly torn apart when the city cantons claimed the larger share of the proceeds. In 1481 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagsatzung">Tagsatzung</a> met in Stans, and the hermit Niklaus von der Flüe sent a message whose contents remain unknown. It calmed tempers and led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stans-agreement">Stanser Verkommnis</a>, under which Fribourg and Solothurn were admitted to the Confederation. In 1713 a fire destroyed two thirds of the town, and the square was rebuilt in 1715 with baroque houses.</p><p>In 1798 French troops stormed Stans after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nidwalden">Nidwalden</a> refused the constitution of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helvetic-republic">Helvetic Republic</a>, and Pestalozzi gathered the orphaned children in the Convent of St Klara, until the army needed the building the following year. Stans and Nidwalden joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sonderbund">Sonderbund</a> in 1845 and took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sonderbund-war">Sonderbund War</a> of 1847. The open-air <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landsgemeinde">Landsgemeinde</a> was held there annually until 1997.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Stans is the capital of the canton of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nidwalden">Nidwalden</a>, and its official language is German, though the main spoken language is the local Alemannic Swiss German. Its sights include the Dorfplatz with the Winkelried monument of 1865, the Capuchin monastery founded in 1583 by Melchior Lussy, and the Winkelriedhaus, which houses a museum. The Stanserhornbahn, built in 1893, is one of the oldest mountain railways in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Stans” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The valleys south of Lake Lucerne, Obwalden around Sarnen and Nidwalden around <b>Stans</b>, were rural communities under scattered lordships, including the Habsburgs.</blockquote><a href="/state/canton-of-unterwalden">Canton of Unterwalden</a></li></ul></section>
